{
  "dom_id": "step-q80353",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248149/assets/1b2f70dda865622ace25db16dfa55c50c746a88ec4bbc34c8560dd0f342a02ac.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-{x}^{2}(x+1)(x-2)^{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=-1$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0,2$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at these points.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{II}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{II}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{II}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
